      { title:'Asphyxiation, chilling and slaughter without effective stunning', context:'Documented fishing/aquaculture practices', description:'Fish may be left to die in air, chilled in ice or ice slurry, bled, gutted or otherwise processed without first being rendered unconscious.', welfare:'Reviews report prolonged aversive responses with several methods; properly applied electrical or percussive stunning can produce rapid unconsciousness for suitable species.', urls:[{label:'Evidence / welfare source',url:'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25000798/'}] },
    ],
    'animal-shellfish': [
      { title:'Crowding, capture and live transport', context:'Species and fishery dependent', description:'Crustaceans and mollusks can be trapped, trawled, sorted, stored and transported alive, sometimes for long periods.', welfare:'Evidence and welfare needs differ substantially across taxa; decapod crustaceans have stronger evidence relevant to pain than many mollusks.', urls:[{label:'Evidence / welfare source',url:'https://www.lse.ac.uk/news/latest-news-from-lse/2021/k-november-21/strong-evidence-that-crustaceans-feel-pain'}] },
      { title:'Boiling, splitting and electrical stunning', context:'Decapod crustaceans; culinary/slaughter practices vary', description:'Lobsters and crabs may be placed alive into boiling water, mechanically split or electrically stunned before killing.', welfare:'Where animals remain conscious, tissue-damaging heat or mechanical procedures create serious welfare concerns; effective stunning aims to abolish sensibility first.', urls:[{label:'Evidence / welfare source',url:'https://www.lse.ac.uk/news/latest-news-from-lse/2021/k-november-21/strong-evidence-that-crustaceans-feel-pain'}] },
      { title:'Important evidence boundary', context:'Taxon-dependent', description:'This entry should not treat every shellfish species as neurologically equivalent. Bivalves such as oysters and mussels have much simpler nervous systems, and evidence for sentience is substantially weaker and more uncertain than for decapod crustaceans and cephalopods.', welfare:'The app should preserve this uncertainty rather than claiming settled evidence where it does not exist.', urls:[{label:'Evidence / welfare source',url:'https://www.lse.ac.uk/business/consulting/reports/review-of-the-evidence-of-sentience-in-cephalopod-molluscs-and-decapod-crustaceans'}] },
