    'animal-bees': [
      { title:'Drone semen collection for instrumental breeding', context:'Instrumental insemination and research', description:'Male honey bees (drones) can be captured, restrained or anesthetized and manually stimulated to ejaculate. Documented methods apply pressure to the thorax and abdomen to force the endophallus to evert, after which semen is collected with a syringe or capillary. Training material notes that the pressure required for partial eversion can range from slight pressure to crushing the head and thorax.', welfare:'The procedure is invasive and can be destructive. The literature documents the physical procedure clearly, but does not provide a reliable overall mortality percentage for routine manual semen collection, so this entry does not invent one.', urls:[{label:'Peer-reviewed semen collection study',url:'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11203891/'},{label:'Instrumental insemination training — Washington State University',url:'https://news.cahnrs.wsu.edu/videos/instrumental-insemination-of-honey-bee-queens-techniques-and-beekeeping-practices-with-susan-cobey/'}] },
      { title:'Lethal drone dissection for semen and reproductive research', context:'Documented laboratory/research methods', description:'Some protocols deliberately kill drones to obtain semen or reproductive tissues. Published methods include decapitation, removal of wings and legs, pinning through the thorax, opening the abdomen and removing the seminal vesicles. Other reproductive studies anesthetize and dissect drones and remove reproductive organs for analysis.', welfare:'These particular methods are explicitly lethal. They should not be generalized to every semen collection procedure, but they demonstrate that destructive drone use is documented in honey-bee research and breeding work.', urls:[{label:'Peer-reviewed destructive semen collection method',url:'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11203891/'},{label:'Peer-reviewed drone reproductive dissection study',url:'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6358831/'}] },
      { title:'Instrumental insemination of queens', context:'Selective breeding and research', description:'Instead of mating freely in flight, a queen can be physically restrained and instrumentally inseminated with semen collected from selected drones. Protocols can involve anesthesia or immobilization and specialized instruments that open the queen reproductive tract so semen can be deposited.', welfare:'Instrumental insemination gives humans direct control over reproduction. Procedures and anesthesia vary, so this should be described as a breeding technique rather than assumed to cause the same outcome in every queen.', urls:[{label:'Peer-reviewed artificial insemination methods',url:'https://elifesciences.org/articles/45009'},{label:'Honey-bee semen preservation and instrumental insemination',url:'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28535305/'}] },
      { title:'Queen replacement, confinement and wing clipping', context:'Colony and swarm management; practice varies', description:'Beekeepers may replace an established queen with a selected queen, confine queens in introduction or shipping cages, and in some operations clip part of a forewing to restrict successful flight during swarming. Queen replacement can require locating and removing the existing queen before the replacement is introduced.', welfare:'These interventions restrict or control the queen for colony productivity, genetics or swarm management. They are not universal to every beekeeper, and natural queen supersedure should be distinguished from deliberate human replacement.', urls:[{label:'Queen-management guidance — University of Florida',url:'https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/IN1457'}] },
      { title:'Drone-brood trapping and deliberate killing', context:'Varroa mite management', description:'Because Varroa mites preferentially reproduce in drone brood, beekeepers can encourage drones to develop in dedicated comb, wait until the brood is capped, then remove it before adult drones emerge. Documented management instructions include freezing combs to kill both mites and developing drones, or cutting out and destroying infested drone brood.', welfare:'This intentionally kills developing male bees as part of parasite control. Varroa itself is a serious threat to colonies, so the management goal is protective at the colony level even though the method deliberately kills the trapped brood.', urls:[{label:'Peer-reviewed drone-brood removal study',url:'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16022287/'},{label:'Varroa management — University of Nevada',url:'https://extension.unr.edu/publication.aspx?PubID=2156'},{label:'Varroa management — Mississippi State University',url:'https://www.extension.msstate.edu/publications/managing-varroa-mites-honey-bee-colonies'}] },
      { title:'Queen-cell removal and swarm control', context:'Swarm-management systems; practice varies', description:'Swarm-control programs can involve repeated hive inspections and removal of developing queen cells so new queens do not emerge as part of the colony’s normal swarming process. Other strategies manipulate colony space, brood or queen movement.', welfare:'Swarming is natural colony reproduction. Human swarm control deliberately alters that reproductive process, although exact methods vary substantially between operations.', urls:[{label:'Queen-management guidance — University of Florida',url:'https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/IN1457'}] },
      { title:'Honey removal and replacement feeding', context:'Honey production; management varies', description:'Beekeepers remove honey stored by the colony for human use. Colonies may then receive sucrose syrup, fondant or other supplemental feed when stores or forage are inadequate.', welfare:'Supplemental feeding is not inherently harmful and can prevent starvation. Nutritional consequences depend on how much honey remains, forage availability, season, colony condition and the replacement diet, so the app does not claim that every honey harvest starves bees.', urls:[{label:'Honey-bee health information — USDA ARS',url:'https://www.ars.usda.gov/oc/br/ccd/index/'}] },
      { title:'Migratory commercial pollination and transport', context:'Commercial pollination', description:'Managed colonies can be loaded onto trucks and transported long distances between crops for pollination. Transportation exposes colonies to confinement and changing temperature and environmental conditions.', welfare:'Peer-reviewed research has documented temperature stress, population losses and physiological effects associated with transportation, with weaker colonies particularly vulnerable. Transport is one of several interacting commercial-management stressors rather than the sole cause of colony losses.', urls:[{label:'Peer-reviewed transportation study',url:'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6554651/'}] },
      { title:'Parasite and disease treatments', context:'Managed colonies; treatment-dependent', description:'Beekeepers may use chemical or nonchemical interventions against Varroa mites and other threats. These treatments are intended to protect colonies from parasites and disease.', welfare:'This is not accurately described as simple abuse: untreated Varroa can devastate colonies. Some control methods can nevertheless have toxic or sublethal effects depending on chemical, dose and exposure, so benefits and treatment risks should both be considered.', urls:[{label:'Varroa management research',url:'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16022287/'}] },
      { title:'Natural mating is fatal to successful drones', context:'Natural biology — not a human-caused beekeeping practice', description:'A successful drone normally dies after natural mating with a queen. During mating the endophallus is everted and reproductive structures are damaged or separated after semen transfer.', welfare:'This belongs here as biological context so natural drone death is not falsely attributed to beekeepers. It is separate from human semen-collection and destructive research procedures.', urls:[{label:'Honey-bee reproductive biology',url:'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13592-019-00684-x'}] },
      { title:'Evidence boundary: avoid unsupported generalizations', context:'Research standard', description:'Individual bees can be injured or killed during hive manipulation, but strong evidence was not found for a universal claim that ordinary honey extraction routinely kills large numbers of bees. Likewise, evidence of high commercial colony losses should not automatically be described as deliberate seasonal extermination.', welfare:'Go Vegan distinguishes documented practices from assumptions. Where prevalence, mortality or causation has not been established, the app says so rather than converting a plausible concern into a factual claim.', urls:[{label:'Honey-bee health information — USDA ARS',url:'https://www.ars.usda.gov/oc/br/ccd/index/'}] },
